CH = dict(num=7, title="Work, Energy and Simple Machines", tag="Physics", theme="Work, work–energy theorem, kinetic and potential energy, conservation, power, pulley, inclined plane, lever",
intro="When forces change with time or act in complicated ways, Newton’s laws become awkward. **Work, energy and power** give a simpler route. This chapter defines **work** scientifically, introduces the **work–energy theorem**, **kinetic and potential energy**, **conservation of mechanical energy**, **power**, and the three **simple machines** — pulley, inclined plane and lever — that make tasks easier without reducing the total work.",
big_questions=["What is the speed of a child at the bottom of a slide?", "Do two children of different masses reach the bottom of the same slide with the same velocity?", "Which slide gives the largest speed at the bottom?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "7.1 Work Done by a Constant Force"),
("ul", ["Lifting a 5 kg bag by 1 m takes a force **mg** upward over 1 m. Lifting 3 bags one after another, or together with 3 times the force, gives 3 times the work; lifting one bag to 3 m also gives 3 times the work (and a machine would use 3 times the fuel).",
 "So work increases proportionally with both the **force** and the **displacement**. Always state which force (agency) does the work and on which object."]),
("note", "FORMULA", "**W = F × s**   (s = displacement in the direction of force).   SI unit **joule (J)**.   **1 J = 1 N × 1 m = 1 kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>−2</sup>** — the work done when a constant force of 1 N displaces an object by 1 m in the direction of the force."),
("ul", ["Work has no direction (it is a scalar) but can be positive or negative.",
 "On a **force–displacement graph**, work = **area under the graph**, even when the force varies (e.g. 10 N × 1 m = 10 J for a rectangle)."]),
("h3", "When is work zero?"),
("table", ["Situation", "Reason", "Example"], [
 ["Force F = 0", "No force", "Object moving with no force on it"],
 ["Displacement s = 0", "Nothing moves", "Pushing a rigid wall; a weightlifter holding a barbell steady (muscles tire from internal energy use, but no work is done on the object)"],
 ["Force **perpendicular** to displacement", "No displacement along the force", "Girl carrying a box horizontally: upward force does no work"]]),
("h3", "Positive and negative work"),
("table", ["", "Positive work", "Negative work"], [
 ["Direction", "Displacement **along** the force", "Displacement **opposite** to the force"],
 ["Effect", "Energy of the object increases", "Energy of the object decreases"],
 ["Examples", "Pushing a wheelchair; lifting a dumbbell up; friction on a body that helps it move", "Goalkeeper stopping a ball; lowering a dumbbell; **friction on a sliding body (the stack of coins)**"]]),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Result"], [
 ["Goalkeeper’s hand moves back 15 cm while stopping a ball with 200 N", "W = 200 × (−0.15)", "−30 J (negative work)"],
 ["Girl lifting and lowering a dumbbell", "Up: force and displacement same direction; down: opposite", "Positive on the way up, negative on the way down"]]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "Forces occur in pairs (third law), so both bodies do work on each other: the goalkeeper does negative work on the ball, while the ball does positive work on the goalkeeper."),

("h2", "7.2 The Work–Energy Theorem"),
("ul", ["A thrown ball can knock down wickets; a raised flowerpot can damage things below. An object with the **capacity to do work** has **energy**.",
 "Positive work on an object increases its energy, which it can later transfer to another object (the ball hands energy to the wickets). Carrom: the striker does positive work on the white coin and the white coin does negative work on the striker, and so on down the chain.",
 "Energy can also transfer as **heat** (from hotter to colder bodies), by **radiation** (Sun to Earth), in electric circuits, by sound waves and by nuclear reactions."]),
("note", "FORMULA", "**Work–energy theorem:** work done on an object (or system) = change in its energy.  Valid also for varying forces and systems.  SI unit of energy = joule. The unit is named after **James Prescott Joule**, who studied how mechanical and thermal energy are related."),

("h2", "7.3 Forms of Energy"),
("table", ["Form", "Meaning"], [
 ["Mechanical", "Due to motion or position of objects"], ["Thermal", "Makes things warm or hot"], ["Light", "Lets us see"], ["Sound", "Energy of vibrations of air or other molecules"],
 ["Electrical", "Related to the position or motion of charges"], ["Nuclear", "Stored in the nuclei of atoms"], ["Chemical", "Stored in fuels and food as bonds between atoms"]]),
("ul", ["Energy changes from one form to another: bulb (electrical → light); water heater (electrical → thermal); food (chemical → mechanical in muscles); bell (mechanical → sound).",
 "Further examples: truck moving uphill (chemical → kinetic and potential); unwinding watch spring (elastic potential → kinetic); photosynthesis (light → chemical); water from a dam (potential → kinetic → electrical); burning match (chemical → heat and light); fire cracker (chemical → heat, light, sound, kinetic); microphone (sound → electrical); electric bulb (electrical → light and heat); solar panel (light → electrical)."]),

("h2", "7.4 Mechanical Energy"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Mechanical energy** = energy due to motion (kinetic) or position/configuration (potential). Mechanical energy = K + U."),
("h3", "Kinetic energy (K)"),
("ul", ["Energy possessed because of motion. A body at rest has zero kinetic energy. It has **no direction**; SI unit joule.",
 "**Derivation:** W = F × s = ma × (v² − u²)/2a = ½m(v² − u²). For u = 0, change in energy = kinetic energy."]),
("note", "FORMULA", "**K = ½ m v²**.   Doubling the velocity makes K **four times** (K is proportional to v²). Positive work → velocity and K increase; negative work → they decrease; zero work → K unchanged."),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Result"], [
 ["Cricket ball 0.2 kg at 154.8 km h<sup>−1</sup> (43 m s<sup>−1</sup>)", "½ × 0.2 × 43²", "184.9 J"],
 ["Jet of 15,000 kg stopped in 100 m by wire force 367,500 N", "½ × 15000 × v² = 367500 × 100 → v² = 4900", "v = 70 m s<sup>−1</sup> = 252 km h<sup>−1</sup>"],
 ["Masses m and 4m with same K", "½mv<sub>A</sub>² = ½(4m)v<sub>B</sub>²", "v<sub>A</sub> : v<sub>B</sub> = 2 : 1"],
 ["Constant velocity at different positions", "v unchanged", "K does not change with position"],
 ["10 kg block with K = 180 J", "½ × 10 × v² = 180", "v = 6 m s<sup>−1</sup>"]]),
("h3", "Potential energy (U)"),
("ul", ["Energy stored by an object because of **deformation** (stretched rubber band/slingshot, bent bow, compressed spring) or by a **system of objects** because of their relative positions (two magnets, two charges, ball and Earth).",
 "Work done in deforming or separating is stored and released as kinetic energy when let go. In internal forces like friction, work done against them is **not** stored as potential energy.",
 "**Activity 7.1:** a heavy ball dropped on a sand bed from 1 m and 2 m makes a deeper dent from the greater height — greater height means greater potential energy (more work done to lift it)."]),
("note", "FORMULA", "**Gravitational potential energy U = mgh** (work to lift an object slowly through h with force mg; U taken as zero on the ground). Valid near the Earth’s surface, where g is nearly constant (≈ 9.8, or 10 m s<sup>−2</sup>). Moving horizontally at constant height does not change U."),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Result"], [
 ["Ball 200 g thrown to 10 m (g = 10)", "0.2 × 10 × 10", "20 J"],
 ["Student 50 kg to height 72.5 m (g = 10) by lift or by stairs", "50 × 10 × 72.5", "36,250 J in both; U depends only on height, not on the path"]]),
("h3", "Conservation of mechanical energy"),
("ul", ["For a freely falling body: at the top, K = 0, U = mgh, total = mgh. After time t: v = gt, fall = ½gt², so K = ½m g²t², U = mgh − ½m g²t². The total is again **mgh**.",
 "Loss in potential energy = gain in kinetic energy; **mechanical energy stays constant** if no other external force (friction, air resistance) acts.",
 "**Pendulum (Activity 7.2):** at extremes P and R the bob has only potential energy (mgh); at the lowest point Q only kinetic energy. It reaches almost the same height each side; it finally stops because of friction at the support and air resistance. Similarly, successive peaks of a ball-roller-coaster exhibit become lower because of friction losses.",
 "Total energy of an isolated system (all forms) stays constant."]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Slide problem:** mgh = ½mv² gives **v = √(2gh)**. The speed at the bottom depends only on the height h, not on the child’s mass or the shape of the slide (friction neglected)."),
("table", ["Example", "Working", "Result"], [
 ["Truck 10,000 kg at 20 m s<sup>−1</sup> on escape ramp at 30°, sand force 50,000 N", "K = ½ × 10⁴ × 400 = 2×10⁶ J; rise = d/2, so mgh = 50,000 d; −50,000 d = 50,000 d − 2×10⁶", "d = 20 m"],
 ["Coconut 1.5 kg falls 10 m (g = 10)", "v = √(2gh); energy 150 J; depth = 150 ÷ 3000", "v ≈ 14.1 m s<sup>−1</sup>; depression 0.05 m"],
 ["Ball 2 kg thrown up at 20 m s<sup>−1</sup>, reaches 19.4 m", "K = 400 J, U = 388 J; difference", "Air resistance did −12 J. Gravity does negative work on the way up, positive on the way down"],
 ["Ball thrown up the same velocity on the Moon (g ≈ 1/6)", "h is proportional to 1/g", "6 × 8 m = 48 m"]]),

("h2", "7.5 Power"),
("note", "FORMULA", "**P = W ÷ t**  (rate of doing work). SI unit **watt (W)**; **1 W = 1 J s<sup>−1</sup>**. 1 horsepower (hp) = 746 W. Named after **James Watt**, who invented an efficient steam engine."),
("ul", ["Running up the stairs in 1 minute and walking up in 5 minutes involve the same work but different power.",
 "More work in the same time, or the same work in less time, needs more power."]),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Power"], [
 ["Weightlifter lifts 75 kg by 2 m in 5 s (g = 10)", "W = 75 × 10 × 2 = 1500 J; ÷ 5", "300 W"],
 ["Car 1000 kg from rest to 20 m s<sup>−1</sup> in 10 s", "W = ½ × 1000 × 400 = 200,000 J; ÷ 10", "20,000 W"],
 ["Crane lifts m to 10th floor in t; then to 20th floor in 2t", "Energy doubles (2E); power = 2E ÷ 2t", "Needs twice the energy, but the same power"],
 ["Man (60 kg) + scooter (100 kg) vs with son (40 kg) added; same v in same time", "K is proportional to mass: 160 : 200", "Fuel ratio 4 : 5"]]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "Raising a flag by a pulley: the work depends on the weight and height, not on speed. Doubling the speed halves the time, so **power doubles**."),

("h2", "7.6 Simple Machines"),
("ul", ["The total work cannot be reduced, but a machine can make it easier by changing the **magnitude or direction** of the force.",
 "**Effort** = force we apply; **load** = force to be overcome. **Mechanical advantage (MA) = load ÷ effort**.",
 "Machines do not create energy; ignoring friction, work put in = useful work on the load (conservation of energy)."]),
("table", ["Machine", "Working", "MA", "Notes / examples"], [
 ["Fixed pulley", "Grooved wheel with rope; changes direction of effort (pull down to lift up)", "1", "Flag-hoisting"],
 ["Movable pulley / pulley system", "Load on movable pulley, one rope end fixed", "> 1", "Cranes, elevators"],
 ["Inclined plane", "Push a load along a slope of length L to height h: effort F′ × L = mgh", "L ÷ h (> 1)", "Longer, gentler slope gives smaller effort over a larger distance; ramps, winding hill roads, inclined ladders"],
 ["Lever", "Rigid bar rotating about a fulcrum: effort × effort arm = load × load arm", "effort arm ÷ load arm", "Crowbar, scissors, seesaw, spoon opening a can lid"]]),
("h3", "Inclined plane"),
("ul", ["Activity 7.3 (spring balance, cart, plank): lifting vertically needs force = weight; pulling up a plank needs less force, and an even less steep (longer) plank needs still less, but the force acts over a longer distance. Work is the same (ignoring friction).",
 "Ramp over a 30 cm step with 40 cm width: length = 50 cm (right triangle), MA = L ÷ h = 50 ÷ 30 = **1.67**.",
 "Winding hill roads and inclined ladders are easier because a longer path means smaller force at each step."]),
("h3", "Lever"),
("ul", ["Parts: **fulcrum** (pivot), **load**, **effort**; **load arm** and **effort arm** are their distances from the fulcrum.",
 "A small force over a large distance on the effort side gives a large force over a small distance on the load side: **F<sub>1</sub>d<sub>1</sub> = F<sub>2</sub>d<sub>2</sub>** (work same). Balance condition (beam balance, Activity 7.5): **effort × effort arm = load × load arm**. MA = effort arm ÷ load arm.",
 "Seesaw: a 15 kg child at 2 m balances a 30 kg child at 1 m (15 × 2 = 30 × 1).",
 "Using a spoon to open a can lid gives a long effort arm; objects to be cut in scissors are placed close to the fulcrum so that the load arm is short."]),
("table", ["Class", "Position", "Examples"], [
 ["Class I", "Fulcrum between effort and load", "Scissors, crowbar, pliers, seesaw, balance scale"],
 ["Class II", "Load between fulcrum and effort", "Lemon squeezer, wheelbarrow, bottle opener"],
 ["Class III", "Effort between fulcrum and load", "Tongs, tweezers, broom, hammer, oar"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Perpetual motion machines are impossible:** real machines lose energy to friction and air resistance, so they eventually stop; energy cannot be created. Water mills (gharat/panchakki) in the Himalayas turn the potential energy of water to kinetic energy to rotate a wheel that grinds grain; dams generate electricity the same way."),
("ul", ["Scientists have proposed **dark energy**, a mysterious form that cannot be exchanged with other forms, to explain the accelerating expansion of the Universe."]),

("qa", [
 ("Define work. State its formula and SI unit.", "Work = force × displacement in the direction of force; joule (1 J = 1 N m)."),
 ("When is work done zero? Give three cases.", "F = 0, s = 0 (pushing a wall), or force perpendicular to displacement (carrying a box horizontally)."),
 ("When is work negative? Give an example.", "When displacement is opposite to force, e.g. goalkeeper stopping a ball, friction on a moving body."),
 ("State the work–energy theorem.", "The work done on an object equals the change in its energy."),
 ("Derive K = ½mv².", "W = F s with F = ma and s = (v² − u²)/2a gives W = ½m(v² − u²); with u = 0, K = ½mv²."),
 ("Derive U = mgh.", "Lifting slowly needs force mg through height h, so work and stored energy = mgh."),
 ("What happens to K if velocity is doubled?", "It becomes 4 times."),
 ("State the law of conservation of mechanical energy with an example.", "Without external forces K + U is constant; in free fall U decreases by the same amount as K increases."),
 ("Define power, unit, and 1 hp.", "Rate of doing work, P = W/t, watt (J s<sup>−1</sup>); 1 hp = 746 W."),
 ("What is mechanical advantage? What is it for a fixed pulley?", "Load ÷ effort; 1 (it only changes direction)."),
 ("State the principle of a lever.", "Effort × effort arm = load × load arm."),
 ("Why can a machine not give us extra work?", "Work done on the load cannot exceed the work put in; energy cannot be created, and friction wastes some.")]),
],
takeaways=["Work W = F × s (joule); no work if the force is zero, there is no displacement or the force is perpendicular to displacement; it can be positive or negative.",
 "Work done on an object equals the change in its energy (work–energy theorem).",
 "Kinetic energy = ½mv²; gravitational potential energy = mgh; mechanical energy (K + U) is conserved when only gravity acts.",
 "Speed at the bottom of a frictionless slide, v = √(2gh), depends only on height.",
 "Power = work ÷ time (watt); 1 hp = 746 W.",
 "Simple machines (pulley, inclined plane, lever) change the magnitude or direction of effort; mechanical advantage = load ÷ effort; total work is never reduced."])
